  NOTES: You can NOT write to the CDROM.
 Be sure you are in a working directory where the files can be installed.

 For Unix files:
  The filenames have been shortened to comply with
  ISO-9660 standards.     RSICC code names such as
  d00189allcp00.tar.Z have been changed to d189tar0.z
 You need to uncompress then tar extract the unix "*.z" file.
 Copy the file(s) to a working directory on your hard drive,
 Change to that directory and "uncompress *.Z", then "tar xvf file".
 If the file ends in ".gz", it must be uncompressed using
 the GNU "gunzip" command.

 To extract the files from the CD-R Disc when disk space is limited,Try:
zcat /cdrom/d189tar0 | tar xvf -
 Please note the ".z" was left off, as the files list as lowercase.z
 but can be extracted as uppercase or aNy ComBINaTioN.z unless the
 exact syntax is required as: cat /cdrom/"README.RSI;1"
 You may wish to: mount -t cdfs -o noversion /dev/cdrom /cdr to
 override the version/extension, OR alternately:
 create a soft link to the file on /cdrom
ln -s /cdrom/"D189TAR0.Z;1" Local.Z (or)
ln -s /cdrom/D189TAR0.Z\;1 Local.Z  (then)
zcat Local.Z | tar xvf -     (and) rm local.Z
 The ISO-9660, HFS, High Sierra, and Rock Ridge specifies the use of the
 extension fields.
 
 Unix users have several other options for DOS format:
 1)AIX users can read the CD with the dosread command.  Other unix systems
   may have similar utilities.
 2)Read files directly under Unix and the run "tr" to delete the ctrl-M's.
   (see help- man translate  ie. tr -d '\015'  <in  >out
 3)From PC, run ftp using ASCII mode to put the needed files on Unix.

 DOS users may extract the files with a tar utility (available with TCP/IP
 software or other source) then use the FLIP utility which can be used to
 translate the end-of-line characters.  FLIP1 and tar4dos are freeware
 available from the simtel.net on the WWW at
                http://oak.oakland.edu/simtel.net/msdos.

 For DOS files:
   The files may be distributed in binary compressed form.
 You need to decompress the '*.EXE' file (files) to get all
 the necessary files. To perform decompression, place the
 distribution diskette in drive A.  Be sure you are in the
 working directory where the files should be installed.
 For example if 'program.exe' is a compressed file, then type
A:program -d
 Extract to your hard disk by executing .EXE with -d switch
 to restore the directory structure.
 The program will list the files being unpacked.  The compression
 was performed using the pkzip utility from PKware, Inc.

 Note some of the documents are in Portable Document Format (pdf) for viewing
 with the Adobe Acrobat Reader which is available for free from Adobe for many
 platforms.  To view these files on-line, you must have the Acrobat Reader
 installed on your computer:
                "http://www.adobe.com/acrobat/readstep.html"
 You may need to configure the helper application. From the Netscape menu bar
 choose: Options, Preferences, Helper Applications, Mime Type: application,
 Mime Subtype: pdf, Extensions: pdf, Action: Launch Application, Click on
 Browse and select Acroread as the application to launch, then press OK.
 Warning: If Acrobat Reader is NOT installed as a viewer on your computer,
 your browser will probably ask if you want the file stored on your hard disk.

 Users with internet access may now get updated package information on the
 ORNL ftp server via anonymous ftp to "ftp://infosrv1.ctd.ornl.gov/pub/rsic/",
 specify binary or ascii mode and get the file(s).
                                        
